{% set local_problem %}
<p>Many Cullman homes have downspout issues that compromise otherwise well-maintained gutter systems. Older homes often have original downspout configurations that were adequate when installed but don't meet current rainfall patterns. Newer homes may have minimum-code installations that overflow during heavy storms.</p>

<p>Common problems we see include: too few downspouts for gutter length, undersized downspouts that can't handle heavy flow, missing or broken extensions that deposit water against foundations, and downspouts positioned where they damage landscaping.</p>

<p>These issues are often straightforward to fix. Adding downspouts, upsizing existing ones, or properly extending water discharge can solve drainage problems without replacing entire gutter systems.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Effective downspout systems have three elements: adequate capacity, proper positioning, and appropriate extensions.</p>

<p>Capacity means having enough downspouts, sized to handle your roof's water volume. North Alabama receives 55+ inches of rain annually — your downspouts need to handle heavy storms, not just average rain.</p>

<p>Positioning means placing downspouts at corners and low points where water naturally collects. Extensions (or underground drains) then carry water well away from your foundation and landscaping.</p>

<p>For historic homes, we offer round downspouts that complement half-round gutters, maintaining architectural integrity while providing modern performance.</p>
{% endset %}

